  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nRemnant activity from this morning continues to dissipate as it \nmoves into eastern Kansas/western Missouri. A persistent layer of \ndry air in the low-levels has prevented any appreciable \naccumulations of rainfall through the day, but some automated \nweather sites have reported light rain through the late morning and \nearly afternoon. Otherwise, a persistent shield of clouds from the \ndecaying complex of showers will prevent temperatures from rising \nconsiderably through the afternoon, keeping them within the 70s for \nmuch of the area. There is the potential for areas outside of the \ncloud shield to heat up more than their surroundings by a few \ndegrees.\n\nCurrent RAP analyses show the beginnings of a shortwave developing \noff the lee of the Rockies, which will be the focal point of our \nnext opportunity for rain tonight. As the shortwave traverses \neastward, a complex of showers/thunderstorms is forecast to form in \ncentral KS out ahead of the shortwave along an axis of dCVA/WAA. \nWith this area of broad synoptic scale lift, stratiform rainfall is \nexpected to overtake eastern KS/western MO a little after midnight \ntonight, with amounts of 1-2\" expected areawide through the duration \nof the event. However, a sticking point with this forecast is an \nanomalously moist environment (PWATS 2-2.5\") amidst deep warm cloud \ndepths (>10 kft), with the potential for greater shear alongside the \nLLJ promoting organized thunderstorm development. If thunderstorms \nare able to develop, they would be highly efficient precipitation \nproducers, which could locally exacerbate rainfall amounts. There is \nsome hint of this in probabilistic guidance, with higher pockets of \n1-hour accumulations in the NBM and swaths of up to 5\" in the 24-\nhour HREF LPMM. With any higher rainfall amounts, localized \nhydrologic issues/flooding would become a greater concern. However, \nwith less confidence in the exact placement of the heaviest axis of \nprecipitation, this will be something that will need to be monitored \nas the event evolves. Additionally, rain is expected to persist \nthrough the day tomorrow as the shortwave continues progressing \neastward, which will contribute to higher totals overall for the \nduration of the event.\n\nAs the shortwave progresses out of the area through Saturday, the \ngreater axis of precipitation will shift off towards the southeast \nas we enter the lee side of the shortwave. Upper-level ridging is \nforecasted to build back in through the weekend as surface high \npressure slides off to the southeast, bringing back warm, humid \nconditions across the area and drying the forecast out. Heat indices \nare forecast to reach the triple digits once again, with \nprobabilities of exceeding heat advisory criteria (105 degF) \nincreasing to 60-80% by Sunday/Monday. With this continued threat of \nexcessive heat, heat headlines will need to be considered within the \nnext few shift cycles. Beyond the renewed heat concerns, there is \nsome potential for precipitation as we ride along the outer fringes \nof the upper-level ridge past the weekend. Presently, any threat of \nappreciable weather appears to remain off towards the \nnorth/northeast. However, with some deviation in deterministic \nguidance in the synoptic solution of the ridge's position, this \ntrend will need to be monitored.",
